  "summary": "Daniel Ferreira (pt-0015): CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast ordered for Mon 10/26 09:00, linked to appt-00001 (sr-t0001). Graded the 2019 iohexol reaction from the radiology report written that day: moderate allergic-like (hives, mild eyelid swelling, no airway or blood-pressure problems). The later \"anaphylaxis\" label on the allergy list came from the patient's own account, so per RBC-RAD-07 it doesn't change the grade. Shellfish and povidone-iodine allergies are not risk factors. Premedication per §2: prednisone 50 mg at Sun 20:00, Mon 02:00 and Mon 08:00 (mr-t0003), plus diphenhydramine 50 mg at Mon 08:00 (mr-t0004). Kidneys (§4, age 64 with diabetes): the ED creatinines (1.94→1.46) were drawn during prerenal AKI and the 9/24 value is more than 30 days old, so a new creatinine is ordered to be drawn before the scan (sr-t0002). The scan was not deferred. Metformin (§6, AKI per the ED summary): mr-00189 is on hold through at least 48 h after the scan. A recheck creatinine is ordered for 10/28–10/30 (sr-t0006), and a task to Dr. Okafor (task-t0007) asks for a decision on restarting. A patient message (comm-t0008) gives the timed instructions, says he needs a driver and must not drive after diphenhydramine, covers the blood draw and stopping metformin, and lists warning signs. A progress note (doc-t0009) documents all of this. I suggest correcting the iohexol allergy entry at the next visit.",
